descriptionPlain:
The draft Law proposes to amend the legislation of Ukraine in the field of medicines, including the Laws of Ukraine “On Medicines” and “On Administrative Services”, in order to establish a new regulatory model while ensuring continuity of relevant state functions and preventing interruptions or undue delays in procedures in the field of medicinal products.The amendments to the Law of Ukraine “On Medicines”, in particular, concern:
shortening the overall timeframe for a decision on the state registration (re-registration) of a medicinal product or refusal of such registration (re-registration) from 210 to 180 calendar days;
improving the provisions concerning the demonstration of bioequivalence of generic medicinal products;
improving the legal framework for clinical trials of medicinal products, including through scientific consultations for applicants on clinical trial-related matters and establishing statutory limits on the cost of the relevant services;
expanding the Bolar exemption by allowing the necessary studies, trials, assessments and other activities required for the state registration of a medicinal product, health technology assessment, state price regulation, reimbursement and preparation for participation in public procurement procedures before the expiry of the relevant patent or supplementary protection certificate (SPC), without allowing the placing on the market or commercial use of the respective medicinal product before the expiry of the relevant intellectual property right;
improving the pharmacovigilance system by regulating the submission and assessment of Periodic Safety Update Reports (PSURs) for medicinal products registered in Ukraine, implementing the European Union Single Assessment (PSUSA) procedure, and introducing the assessment of post-authorisation safety study (PASS) protocols and substantial amendments thereto;
improving the legal regulation of pharmacy and hospital pharmacy activities and state regulation of prices for medicinal products, including by ensuring the functioning of the National Price Catalogue and improving the mechanisms for the declaration and review of prices for medicinal products;
improving mechanisms for responding to risks of shortages of medicinal products and ensuring continuity of their supply through special measures in circumstances that may affect the availability of medicinal products;
improving transitional provisions concerning medicinal products registered before the entry into force of the new legislation and clinical trials authorised before the entry into force of the Law, including by maintaining the possibility of placing on the market medicinal products registered under the legislation previously in force for the period specified by the transitional provisions, and allowing the completion of clinical trials authorised before the entry into force of the Law.
The draft Law also proposes to amend the Law of Ukraine “On Administrative Services” in order to exclude relations in the field of state regulation of the circulation of medicinal products from its scope.

The draft Law of Ukraine “On Biocidal Products” establishes a regulatory framework for the approval of active substances used in biocidal products and the state registration, production, making available on the market, placing on the market and use of biocidal products and treated articles. It also defines the rights and obligations of legal entities and individuals, as well as the powers of state authorities and their officials in the field of biocidal products.Specifically, the draft Law:establishes procedures for the assessment and approval, renewal or review of approval of active substances used in biocidal products, including the preparation of expert conclusions;
establishes a procedure for the state registration of biocidal products, including under a simplified procedure;
establishes the procedure and criteria for authorizing a state entity to conduct the relevant assessments and provide related services, as well as the duration and withdrawal of such authorization and the procedure for determining the fees for the relevant services;
establishes requirements for laboratories conducting laboratory testing of active substances for the preparation of active substance dossiers, including accreditation requirements;
provides for the establishment of the State Register of Biocidal Products, which is to become operational on 1 January 2030, through the modernization of the existing State Register of Disinfectants;
stipulates that all registrations of disinfectants that are valid as of the date of entry into force of the Law shall remain valid until their expiry;establishes the classification of biocidal products, as set out in an Annex to the draft Law.The draft Law also introduces amendments to the Laws of Ukraine “On Protection of the Population from Infectious Diseases” and “On the Public Health System” to ensure consistency of legislation concerning biocidal products.This draft Law was previously notified in 2025 under the title “On Making Available on the Market and Use of Biocidal Products”.The draft Law is also notified in accordance with the requirements of the SPS Agreement.

The Order of the Ministry of Health of Ukraine No. 740 of 3 June 2026 approves a new Procedure for Pharmacovigilance, developed pursuant to the Law of Ukraine No. 2469-IX of 28 July 2022 “On Medicinal Products” and taking into account recommendations for international harmonisation in the field of pharmacovigilance.The Procedure establishes:unified approaches to the establishment and maintenance of pharmacovigilance systems at the state level and by marketing authorisation holders;
requirements for the Pharmacovigilance System Master File (PSMF), which is maintained by the marketing authorisation holder;
minimum requirements for the pharmacovigilance quality system for the state control authority and applicants;
the use of harmonised international terminology, formats and standards in accordance with European practice;
updated requirements for the collection, analysis and monitoring of data on adverse reactions, including through the national pharmacovigilance database;
procedures for the electronic submission of reports on adverse reactions, Periodic Safety Update Reports (PSURs) and Risk Management Plans (RMPs); 
requirements for post-authorisation safety studies, including the form and content of study protocols and final reports.The Order also declares invalid the Order of the Ministry of Health of Ukraine No. 898 of 27 December 2006 “On Approval of the Procedure for Pharmacovigilance”.
